WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.385
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:07.860
FRASER MACDONALD:
I'm Fraser Macdonald.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.270
I'm a product guy at Google
working on Cloud IoT.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.780
So IoT Core as a product,
and a lot of the extended IoT

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.080
ecosystem that Google has
got a lot of offerings in.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.850
I'm really excited to
have you here today

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.770
for a pretty specific topic.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.690
And it's one that when we were
putting this talk together,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.150
we were really excited to do.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.650
Because with a
smaller audience, you

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.320
get a chance to chat about
something that's actionable.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.530
So we want to talk today
about some real specifics

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:34.610
of replatforming.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.859
Because it's a terrifying
thing to a lot of companies,

00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:39.317
but it's a huge mover for
a lot of companies as well.

00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:40.900
So just to get us
kicked off, as a bit

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.390
of an idea of who's sitting in
the audience, how many people

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.930
here have actually gone through
it, some form of technology

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.530
replatforming,
themselves in the past?

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.110
Sure, yeah, a good amount
of the group that's probably

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.750
felt the pains and struggles and
the benefit on the other side.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.410
What about been part
of a company that's

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.050
discussed a replatforming
but decided against it?

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.360
A little bit, yeah.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.280
Those conversations
come up, too.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.130
So we wanted to make sure to
cover some of the specifics

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.770
today, and we're
excited to do it.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.400
So I want to start
by asking you this--

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.690
how many of these
buzzwords and technology

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.060
trends over the years either
strike fear in your heart,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.434
because you were
part of the team

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:26.050
responsible for what are
we going to do about this,

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:30.810
or are a fond memory of
something we've gone through?

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.630
Personally, Y2K is the one
that stands out for me.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.540
I grew up in a house with a
dad who was a disaster recovery

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.710
planner, professionally,
for a bank.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.920
And so Y2K rolled around,
and it was his job

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.380
to make sure that the Canadian
Imperial Bank of Commerce

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.970
didn't go under because of Y2K.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.010
If you can't tell a Canadian
accent, that's where it's from.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.580
So we had a bunker in our
basement full of canned foods.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.020
So I still have associations
of fears of, what's

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.390
going to go wrong with Y2K?

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.220
But each one of these
is a technology trend

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.500
that's happened over
the last 15, 20 years.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.530
And each one of these is
also a real opportunity.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.800
When the rise of mobile
started coming in earnest,

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.289
companies realized they needed
to adopt new strategies so

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.630
that their businesses were
capable of handling mobile.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.340
When e-commerce
started springing up,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.196
companies realized, we need
to do some pretty significant

00:02:15.196 --> 00:02:16.570
technical overhaul
so that we can

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:20.080
make sure we can sell all
of our product online.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.600
And we're kind of getting
to that point in the IoT

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.860
now as well.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.200
As the IoT is getting
more mature and more

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.804
both consumer and industrial
products are launching

00:02:28.804 --> 00:02:30.220
that are connected
to the internet

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.340
either just as sensor networks
or sensors and actuators

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.612
that you're talking back
to, these technologies

00:02:35.612 --> 00:02:36.820
are starting to get codified.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.986
We're starting to get a few
more protocols emerging.

00:02:38.986 --> 00:02:41.260
We're starting to get a
common understanding of what

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.600
are the building blocks
of device management,

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.630
of handling the
data, and how do we

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.437
tie users into the ecosystem.

00:02:48.437 --> 00:02:50.770
And as this is happening,
it's following the same trends

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.600
as a lot of these did.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.060
Companies are realizing,
OK, I started with a POC,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.790
and we put something together
internally that worked,

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.940
and we built or we had an
SI shop that built for us.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.050
But we want to take
it production grade.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.420
So at Google, we're having
a lot of conversations

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.600
about replatforming, and I think
there's a big reason for it.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.140
And that's we're hitting the
point where companies are not

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.607
all green field anymore.

00:03:15.607 --> 00:03:17.440
There are a lot of
companies out there today

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.230
that have some form of
connected product in the market,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.096
it's got a bit of
internet connectivity,

00:03:22.096 --> 00:03:24.220
and they're looking at
going to the next generation

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:24.845
of the product.

00:03:24.845 --> 00:03:26.020
They stuck one out there.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.340
It was a pilot project.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.460
They got success with it.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:29.830
And they're almost victims
of their own success,

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.510
realizing we need
to scale things out.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.343
We need to be able to
roll out a new feature

00:03:33.343 --> 00:03:34.490
that we didn't anticipate.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:35.865
And so a lot of
the conversations

00:03:35.865 --> 00:03:38.350
that we're having at
Google are with companies

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.069
who have something
stood up already

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:41.860
and need to figure out
how to integrate it.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.930
It's a common conversation.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.850
So we're seeing
roughly two camps

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.640
of companies that are going
through replatforming.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.610
One is motivated by the pushes.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.060
It's we built something that
was meant for a couple thousand

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.955
users, and we'd like to be
able to handle a couple hundred

00:03:57.955 --> 00:03:59.170
thousand.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.600
Or one of the major
pieces of technology

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.010
that we are reliant on
is going out of business,

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.890
and we need to
make accommodations

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.470
for this yesterday.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.260
A lot of it also is things are
getting out of hand because we

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.060
didn't build this
to scale, and we're

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:16.029
running our own infrastructure,
and we can't keep the costs up.

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:19.120
Another big push
factor is I'm not sure

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.680
that our technical
team in-house is

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.190
the one that should be focusing
entirely on our IoT product.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:25.440
We need them for other things.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.606
Just as often, there is
something on the other side.

00:04:27.606 --> 00:04:28.670
There's the pull factors.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.260
And that's what we
were talking about.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.510
New technologies are emerging.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:33.860
New standards are emerging.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.770
IoT Core as a product that the
Xively acquisition from January

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.810
and the IoT core
product in Google

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.319
are coming together to
provide is device management

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:43.860
as a service.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:44.620
It's connectivity.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:45.960
It's device security.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.437
It's communication
over known protocols.

00:04:48.437 --> 00:04:50.020
A lot of these
technologies in the IoT

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.725
are starting to
become standards,

00:04:51.725 --> 00:04:53.600
and companies want to
take advantage of that.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.690
I don't want to run
my own message broker.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.120
I don't want to run my own
relationship between users

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.590
and devices.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.270
Similarly, they want
to take advantage

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.000
of the teams that are
building at other companies

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.850
so their own team
doesn't have to.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:09.640
If you can piggyback off of
somebody's real time database

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.000
technology, or you can handle
string processing or analytics

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.830
through a software
as a service tool,

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.207
it means that your team of
two, three, 10 engineers

00:05:18.207 --> 00:05:19.790
in-house can focus
on something that's

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.001
just specific to your business.

00:05:22.001 --> 00:05:24.250
So there are a lot of reasons
why this conversation is

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.050
coming up at companies.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.970
But just as often,
there are a lot

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:32.560
of reasons why it's terrifying,
and the conversations are slow.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.470
I had a conversation with
somebody recently who--

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:37.630
the quote from
him was, you know,

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:40.150
replatforming is not a
great way to get promoted,

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.990
but it's an excellent
way to get fired.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.820
And I think it's a
common sentiment.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.270
Because there's a lot of fear
around the idea of taking

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.674
a plane that's flying in the
air and swapping out the engine,

00:05:52.674 --> 00:05:54.340
taking a product
that's already launched

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:55.780
in your customers' hands.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.162
And you know that you
can get better out of it,

00:05:58.162 --> 00:06:00.370
but you're really afraid of
touching something that's

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:01.709
already out there in the field.

00:06:01.709 --> 00:06:02.750
And there are real risks.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.375
You don't want to be
the company that's

00:06:04.375 --> 00:06:07.840
on the front of the newspaper
because your product shut off.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.420
Somebody pushed a firmware
image that they shouldn't

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:14.320
have had permission to push,
and they bricked 10,000 devices.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.620
Or you don't want to be the
company that's wasting time

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.780
and gets the call
from your exec that

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.150
says, why is my
engineering team working

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.980
on building a new device
connectivity layer?

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.436
Don't we already have that?

00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:28.810
And you don't have
a good answer.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.722
What's the ROI of why we're
switching over to something?

00:06:31.722 --> 00:06:34.180
So we wanted to take some time
when we are putting together

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.450
the talk today to address
these points head on.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.160
They're real, practical
considerations,

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.370
and they don't often get
talked about directly.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.860
So we're hoping to do that
in a bit of a unique format.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.443
What we're going to
do is talk through

00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:48.340
some of the best
practices that Rick

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.381
from Augusta, who is going
to come up and join me

00:06:50.381 --> 00:06:53.410
in a minute as an SI, and
myself working on the platform

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.125
side of IoT have seen emerge.

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:56.500
When companies
are going through,

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.170
what are the best practices?

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.760
What are some of the things
you should take away?

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.605
And Rick's going to walk us
through a couple of examples

00:07:03.605 --> 00:07:05.980
of companies that have actually
been there and done that,

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.590
and how they made it happen.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.600
Because tackling these problems
should not be enormous,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.650
and while they should
weigh all of these risks,

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.850
these risks should not stop
you from getting the benefits.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.950
So I'm going to invite
Rick up, and let's

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:22.480
take a look at a couple
of these best practices.

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.230
So the first one we're
going to talk about

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.880
is Run a Tracer Bullet.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.380
And for those who aren't
familiar with the expression,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:32.660
a tracer bullet's meant to
indicate, what's something

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.587
that we can do end to end?

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:36.170
What's something
that we can carve out

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.770
as a small piece of
our working solution

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:41.870
and go through the
process from start

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.690
to finish with maybe a
subset of our customers,

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:46.451
or maybe one site only?

00:07:46.451 --> 00:07:48.200
Because you're going
to run into problems.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.600
You're going to need to
answer the call at 2:00 AM

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.070
because something's broken.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.440
You're going to
realize that you forgot

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:57.380
that your users had to log in
through a new system instead

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:58.286
of an old system.

00:07:58.286 --> 00:07:59.660
You're going to
realize that, oh,

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.080
yeah, that particular device
isn't capable of holding

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.250
a firmware image that big.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.587
Something is going to go wrong.

00:08:05.587 --> 00:08:07.670
And so one of the best
practices you can take away

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.260
is pick a site.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.910
In an industrial
context, maybe it's

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:12.560
one site that was
an early customer

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.446
and who will be very
forgiving and really

00:08:14.446 --> 00:08:17.070
eager to try out some of the new
technology you're rolling out.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.939
Or if it's a consumer context,
pick a subset of your users

00:08:19.939 --> 00:08:22.480
that might be power users, and
might be a bit more forgiving,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.830
and walk through the
whole process with them.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:29.120
And to give a bit of an idea in
real terms of what we've seen,

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.576
this is what we recommend
for how you stage things out.

00:08:31.576 --> 00:08:34.159
You want to spend a good amount
of time on that Tracer Bullet.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:36.054
Three to six months
should go into--

00:08:36.054 --> 00:08:37.429
after you've had
the conversation

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:40.280
and you've justified why
are we making this change,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.260
how much time should
you be looking

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:43.720
at putting towards this?

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.250
Spend up to six months
with that early customer

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:48.410
to make sure that the
people on your support team

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.420
know what they're doing, the
people on the technical side

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:51.470
know exactly what they're doing.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.095
You've actually gone through the
process of pressing the button

00:08:54.095 --> 00:08:55.309
and feeling the fear.

00:08:55.309 --> 00:08:57.350
Because that's going to
make each of these stages

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.704
down the road work
a lot more smoothly.

00:08:59.704 --> 00:09:01.370
Running in parallel
is just a great idea

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:04.720
in any replatforming and
rolling out any new technology.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.010
It's sometimes thought of
as, you know, blue-green.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:09.204
That you have a live
deployment and you're

00:09:09.204 --> 00:09:11.120
swapping in-- getting
something up and running

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.260
before you're swapping
it into your whole fleet.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:15.260
And finally, the crossover
stage is pretty quick

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:16.950
once you've walked
through all of this.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.940
So I'm going to turn it over to
Rick to talk a bit about a case

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.925
where he's seen exactly this.

00:09:21.925 --> 00:09:23.300
RICK ERICKSON:
Thank you, Fraser.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.550
And thanks everybody
for having me up.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:26.570
I'm Rick Erickson.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.940
I'm actually a chief class
strategist at Augusta,

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.660
and I also run very specifically
our IoT practice area.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.866
And so I've had the
pleasure of working

00:09:34.866 --> 00:09:36.740
with a number of really
interesting companies

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.221
at different stages
of replatform.

00:09:39.221 --> 00:09:41.720
And one of them that I want to
tell you about really quickly

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.580
is a company called Limelight.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.760
This is a very
innovative company.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:48.140
They launched a product
actually about nine years ago

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.360
in the industrial lighting
and control space.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:56.240
They had originally a vision
for taking a zigbee mesh

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.820
and finding use cases they
could implement that mesh in

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:02.780
and ended up partnering
with some very large

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:07.910
lighting control
components and working

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:10.800
with some of the providers
that actually delivered

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.020
these lights at both the
industrial scale, parking,

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:18.740
and some other locations,
and found wild success.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.590
And a very interesting
company in that, you

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:23.270
know, as you describe,
the use cases

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.790
of companies that
sort of tested an idea

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:29.720
and realized some level
of success initially.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:33.620
But then were sort of, as
a result of their success,

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.040
ended up having challenges down
the road because they didn't

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:38.574
scale appropriately
or were stuck

00:10:38.574 --> 00:10:40.490
with an architecture
that was dated and needed

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.320
to be modernized.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.150
Limelight certainly fills that.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.300
So with Limelight, you know,
one of the things that they

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.030
had a challenge
with was, we want

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:53.507
to modernize our entire
stack, but we kind of

00:10:53.507 --> 00:10:54.590
don't know where to start.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.290
And so we also haven't
partnered with anybody outside

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.220
of our engineering
team in the past.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.950
So how do we do
that in a way that

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:04.940
allows us to sort
of test that concern

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.600
but also solve problems
inside our stack of services

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.470
that were the most impactful?

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.590
And so in Limelights
case, they happened

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.890
to have an MVNO that
acquired technology.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.390
This is a technology that had
been acquired by three or four

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:18.830
other companies.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.040
And this acquisition
of technology

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.350
was really no more than
a connection broker.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:28.070
This is sort of in the
world prior to platforms,

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:30.440
and this company had a
proprietary connection

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:32.780
broker that was right in
the middle of their stack.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.960
And they had a lot of
concerns about this stack

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:38.810
going end-of-life
and being stuck with,

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.130
what do we do if these
guys go out of business?

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:45.520
We have hundreds of
customers, huge customers,

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.550
like the parking garage
here at Moscone and others,

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.170
that use our technology.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.270
And these organizations
would literally

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:55.079
have concerns about
configurations

00:11:55.079 --> 00:11:56.870
going offline and being
able to manage them

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.010
if they were end-of-life.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:04.280
And so for them, a big part
of their vision around this

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:09.380
is, how do we validate that
this important plumbing

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:11.900
in between our devices
and our back office

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:16.680
is going to function as
appropriate and as expected?

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.570
So let me tell you
kind of how we did it.

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.247
So I'm not going to get too
deep into the architecture,

00:12:21.247 --> 00:12:23.330
but I want to give you
kind of some sense of where

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:23.930
they were at.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.388
So I mentioned
their initial state.

00:12:25.388 --> 00:12:27.830
I just mentioned they were
using this third-party MVNO.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:31.880
They have a proprietary gateway
that's speaking, basically,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.040
a proprietary protocol.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:38.300
And that server in the
middle is where the MVNO

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.910
is hosting this broker.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.809
And on the edge of the
application services,

00:12:43.809 --> 00:12:45.350
it could read and
interpret that data

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:48.960
coming from these zigbee
devices on the end.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:51.194
And so what we wanted
to do is literally

00:12:51.194 --> 00:12:53.360
kind of think about this
as a canary in a coal mine.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.776
Their use cases are they have
a multi-tenant system that's

00:12:55.776 --> 00:12:56.570
deployed.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:12:59.600
They also have customers that
have regulatory restrictions

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:02.960
that require them to deploy
their entire technology stack

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.050
in an isolated premise cloud.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:09.170
So they have two concerns that
they have to kind of validate

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:09.860
for.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.480
And so what we did
was we actually

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.990
set up a common
ISO-certified broker

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.510
that would run on Cloud
Platform but also be

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:21.230
containerized and capable of
operating in a hybrid scenario,

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.210
as well, or on the prem.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:27.440
So again, fairly simple
problem, but in order

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:29.990
to make sure that we
had literally a tracer

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.509
bullet running from edge
to edge of this system,

00:13:33.509 --> 00:13:36.050
we wanted to validate that there
weren't going to be problems

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:36.807
in both scenarios.

00:13:36.807 --> 00:13:38.390
And they certainly
wanted to determine

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:41.560
that they had the right partner
to help them in this journey.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:46.660
So the end result was we were
able to basically horseshoe

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:51.320
in this broker service,
rewrite the MQTT client

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.930
on the edge for the gateway.

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:57.650
And so we were supporting the
same hardware that was already

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:58.880
living at the edge--

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:02.180
no changes to that
hardware at all--

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:05.720
and then support a client
broker that could also interpret

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:07.460
the messaging coming back.

00:14:07.460 --> 00:14:10.909
And so this was a
service on the edge.

00:14:10.909 --> 00:14:12.200
That was a service on the edge.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:13.890
This was a service
in the back end.

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:17.060
And then we implemented
basically kind

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:21.050
of a bridge that allowed us to
put a canary in the coal mine

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:23.570
and validate that from
the edge to edge we

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:26.150
were able to reduce that risk.

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:27.870
It would function as expected.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.940
And so our first
deployments for them--

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.273
and this is one of the sort
of the learnings from this--

00:14:32.273 --> 00:14:35.840
is deploy at an isolated
environment that doesn't affect

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:37.400
all of your tenants at once.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.710
Have that be your
starting point.

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:40.820
So good learning there.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:43.340
And then, pick
something that is going

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:47.890
to be relatively impactful
but not necessarily impact

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:49.140
all of your customers at once.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.960
And so we took that
initial tracer bullet,

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.880
ran it through,
validated it, and then

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:57.080
moved to the multi-tenant
deployment after that.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:15:00.402
So that was our first
set of learning.

00:15:00.402 --> 00:15:02.840
I'm going to pass this back off.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:03.840
FRASER MACDONALD: Great.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:04.502
Thanks, Rick.

00:15:04.502 --> 00:15:06.960
And I should say, as we're
going through these customer use

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.270
cases, too, we're going
to have an opportunity,

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.106
after we go through
four examples with you,

00:15:12.106 --> 00:15:13.230
to chat with the companies.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:17.490
We're going to have a Q&amp;A
and invite the companies up

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.194
to speak about
their experiences.

00:15:19.194 --> 00:15:20.610
So if you get a
question as you're

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:21.700
going through that
we don't cover,

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:22.741
make sure to jot it down.

00:15:22.741 --> 00:15:25.520
Remember there's going
to be a mic at the end.

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:27.580
So the second one is
understanding your change

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:28.617
tolerance.

00:15:28.617 --> 00:15:30.700
And this is an important
one, especially as you're

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.680
communicating a
replatforming story

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:34.510
through your own organization.

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:37.100
There's a lot of fear
of how big this can get.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:38.950
And it's a reasonable fear.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:41.800
And what we've noticed,
in a couple of rounds

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:44.530
of replatforming
with many companies,

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:46.900
is that there are
roughly three patterns.

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.700
You can go all the way
from light, medium,

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:49.725
through to heavy.

00:15:49.725 --> 00:15:51.850
And you need to know, as
a company going into this,

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:53.710
how strategic is this to us?

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.200
And how much risk
tolerance do we have?

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.120
How much change
tolerance do we have?

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.980
And what's the timeline
going to look like?

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.780
So a replatform does not have
to be an enormous takeover

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.040
all at once.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.310
The light option here is perhaps
replacing a single component,

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:11.200
or supplementing a
single component.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.630
This can be taking an
architecture that's already

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:17.270
using one flavor of MQTT
for device communication

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:20.260
and moving to a different
MQTT device broker.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:23.440
Not much of an interruption,
and you can do it piecemeal.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:26.110
It's a much easier sell if
you're doing it in parts.

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.030
The mid-size ones
are where you're

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:31.210
adding a couple of different
new pieces to the mix.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:33.640
You might need to take
in a new data format.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:37.060
Or you might be working
on live databases.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.560
And this is where
it can get tricky,

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.560
because you're going to have
to be doing some hot swapping.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:46.400
The heaviest of the replatforms
are full re-architectures.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:49.494
And it's almost
never recommended.

00:16:49.494 --> 00:16:51.910
In fact, if you're thinking
about a replatforming problem,

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:53.451
I guarantee you you
can break it down

00:16:53.451 --> 00:16:55.060
into smaller, bite-sized pieces.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:59.230
And we'll talk in a bit as well
that it's probably pretty easy

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.630
if you sit down with you
and your executive team

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:03.580
and the business people
in your organization

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:05.380
to identify, what's
the one thing that's

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:06.629
going to have the most impact?

00:17:06.629 --> 00:17:08.200
You're going to know.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.750
So each of the sizes
of these replatformings

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:13.680
is something that you
have to know as a company.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.550
So Rick, I'm hoping
you can walk us

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:19.359
through an example of exactly
this style of decision-making.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:20.800
RICK ERICKSON: Yeah, you bet.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.020
So let me tell you a
story about-- actually,

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:26.619
we're lucky enough to have
one of the innovators of this

00:17:26.619 --> 00:17:28.870
platform that was developed
by Group ADO here,

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:31.000
and you'll talk to him
when we do our Q&amp;A.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.664
But Group ADO is a
municipal busing--

00:17:35.664 --> 00:17:37.330
both short haul, long
haul, and delivery

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:41.890
busing-- one of the largest
in Latin America and Spain.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:46.330
And they have a really number
of innovation programs that

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:48.790
are built out for
their consumers,

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:51.380
and ways to sort of
track driver behavior,

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:58.510
a series of services that are
called SIIAB that are actually

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:02.770
quite sophisticated and were
built almost 15 years ago now

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:04.480
to support those user needs.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:08.590
And these are outfitted across
the bulk of their fleet.

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:12.070
And one of the
challenges that they had

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:18.910
was opportunities to
add innovative services

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:22.090
across these existing services.

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:23.710
But also, how do
they figure out how

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:27.400
to take this sort of
legacy stack of services

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.860
and implement them in
a more modernized way?

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:34.150
And again-- sophisticated,
complicated, buses-moving

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:35.080
set of services.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:41.480
So let me-- this is our
replatforming patterns.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:43.190
We just covered
that, did we not?

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:44.190
I'm going the wrong way.

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:44.820
Sorry.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:45.600
OK.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:52.510
So what ADO really
wanted to do is

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.510
they've got an innovator
that was thinking about,

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.190
what are the
opportunities for me

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:00.670
to take my existing
stack of services

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:03.760
and implement more innovative
problems using machine

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:06.100
learning and analytics?

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:08.920
And I want to start
with, how can I

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.830
figure out both
the impact of that

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.200
and understand what's capable?

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:17.980
And at the same time,
I've got this legacy stack

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:22.420
of services that are being
used to deliver my existing

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:23.710
product to market.

00:19:23.710 --> 00:19:26.860
I can't blow that up, because
that's got to still operate.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:30.355
And I also need
to consider, where

00:19:30.355 --> 00:19:34.150
are the places throughout my
stack of services, my hardware

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:38.230
stack, that is going to impact
the organization the most?

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:43.990
So at the edge, they've got a
relatively expensive computing

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:50.270
device that effectively will
handle all the communication

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:53.930
between the bus driver, the
systems inside providing

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:57.680
services to consumers
that can pay

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:00.450
using paid services
on a mobile device,

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:03.660
but also provide entertainment
services on the bus

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:06.110
and other behavioral analytics.

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:09.590
And that piece of device is a
proprietary embedded Windows

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:13.820
device that's very expensive
for them to not only source

00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:18.320
but also to maintain
and provide longer-term.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:23.900
So the use case that I
described is not uncommon.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:28.250
We find a lot of companies
that have older and aged

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:29.614
infrastructure at the edge.

00:20:29.614 --> 00:20:32.030
They need to figure out ways
of, how can they replace that

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:35.900
with a lower-cost device
that can be more readily

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:38.660
sourced but also still provide
the same level of compute

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:42.230
and intelligence that they
need to support their existing

00:20:42.230 --> 00:20:44.360
set of services?

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.970
I'm sure that all of you
maybe have had experiences

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:50.090
with using really
dated technology

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:51.160
and seeing how it works.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:53.510
And this is the same
concern that ADO had.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:56.330
So we decided that we would
help them on this journey

00:20:56.330 --> 00:21:00.010
by helping them stand up a
feasibility proof of concept

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:03.740
that would validate that they
could support this larger

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:09.650
vision for advanced services
and be able to also begin

00:21:09.650 --> 00:21:14.390
to start materializing a model
for an edge computing device

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:17.010
that would be much less
expensive to source,

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:20.630
to build materials for,
and also provide a higher

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:24.230
level of performance to support
the vision that, in this case,

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:28.160
Verne and his team had to
validate that this was even

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:29.700
possible.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:32.600
So just to give you some
sense of what we used,

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:37.820
we've got a Raspberry Pi
here running Android Things,

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:40.100
a very lightweight device.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:44.910
This device actually sat
on a number of pilot buses,

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:47.510
and we used this
device at the edge

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:52.860
to effectively replace
this ruggedized Windows PC.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:57.240
So that device was able to
take engine diagnostic data

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:03.940
off the CAN bus, which is where
we basically have a vehicle

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.930
controller that uses a
proprietary protocol,

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:11.050
and then pulls that data
into this CAN card device,

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:14.550
and ultimately pushes this to
our Android Things Raspberry

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:15.109
Pi.

00:22:15.109 --> 00:22:16.650
So if you know
anything about Things,

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:21.090
you know that it's a powerful
piece of hardware that can also

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.610
support, depending on
what implementation of Pi

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.520
you put, but can also
support doing some processing

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:27.250
at the edge.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:31.290
And so our validation here was
proving that we could pull data

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:35.550
into IoT Core, implementing
that through a data engineering

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:38.280
environment, and proving
that we could classify

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:42.780
data to eventually be part of
developing a machine learning

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:48.120
model to support vehicle
prognostics or diagnostic data.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:52.200
And so we completed that project
in a very short period of time,

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:54.450
about four weeks.

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:59.070
And we were able to
settle nerves and concerns

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:04.200
that, number one, a device at
the edge, which is much less

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:07.740
expensive, could even support
a similar type of model that

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.380
was in the visionaries'
head for this,

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:13.140
but also that we could support
the same level of service

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:18.390
quality and prove value
in an innovative service

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:23.190
like prognostics and
machine-learning-type services

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:29.890
to ultimately improve the value
of the product to the market.

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:32.190
And just to give you some
idea of the sophistication

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:37.200
of this vision, ADO actually
built out a comprehensive kind

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.420
of layout here of
what the architecture

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:42.240
could look like in the future.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.520
And this is ideally what I
want you to focus on here

00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:47.240
is that what we're
trying to do, when

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:49.350
you try to validate a
system like this, is really

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:52.920
understand, what's the
most impactful business

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:55.980
solution that you want to
put in front of your users

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:57.570
and/or back to the business?

00:23:57.570 --> 00:24:01.020
And how can you surface
that in the simplest way?

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.520
A three to four-week
project, you're

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.590
not going to solve
the entire problem.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:07.832
But you'll get a very
good sense of the impact

00:24:07.832 --> 00:24:10.290
of how you could deploy this
and how you can implement this

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:13.830
to support a much more
comprehensive business case,

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:17.130
and then take that
learning to how

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:19.000
it fits into your overall
road map strategy.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.030
So we were able to prove
both of these points

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:24.840
on the machine learning side, to
support future SIIAB features,

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:31.170
and also how to use common
analytics services to support

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:33.690
larger business
intelligence, and eventually

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:38.650
surface that to the customer
and back to the business.

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:41.340
All right.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.750
You got that one.

00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:45.678
FRASER MACDONALD: Thanks, Rick.

00:24:45.678 --> 00:24:46.650
OK.

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:50.160
So best practice number three--

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:51.880
partition your problem.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.750
And this is one that we've
talked a bit about already.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:58.890
This is a pretty simple concept
that can save you a ton of time

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:00.540
as you're working
through, what do

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.400
we really want to replatform?

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.260
When you take a look
at an IoT solution,

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:06.510
it's possible to break
it down into pieces.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:10.890
And generally, those pieces
follow some consistent buckets.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:13.930
You've got your devices and
your device communication.

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:16.530
You've got your users
and their applications

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:17.952
and their permissions.

00:25:17.952 --> 00:25:19.660
You've got your
third-party integrations.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:21.868
And then you've got your
data lake and your analytics

00:25:21.868 --> 00:25:22.860
going on in the cloud.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:25.110
And when you take a look at
an architecture like this,

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:26.444
the pieces start to tease apart.

00:25:26.444 --> 00:25:28.859
And so if you're sitting down
with your team and thinking,

00:25:28.859 --> 00:25:30.420
we're not able to
scale fast enough.

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:33.030
Or, it requires a manual
provisioning every time

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:34.111
we install on-site.

00:25:34.111 --> 00:25:35.610
Or, we need to
launch a new feature,

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:37.810
but we don't have an
integration with a partner.

00:25:37.810 --> 00:25:39.630
Or, we're not really
happy with the way

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:41.495
that our apps are
set up right now.

00:25:41.495 --> 00:25:43.620
Chances are you're talking
about one specific piece

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:44.970
of this technical architecture.

00:25:44.970 --> 00:25:47.850
You don't need to rip and
replace absolutely everything.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:49.959
You can be pretty tactical
about how you do it.

00:25:49.959 --> 00:25:51.750
So if you take a look
at this diagram here,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.470
this is an example architecture
for how an IoT product might be

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:58.530
set up on GCP using GCP Tools.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:00.720
On the left-hand side,
you have IoT Core,

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:02.771
which is our product for
connecting to devices.

00:26:02.771 --> 00:26:04.770
And there are a lot of
fantastic products inside

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:06.570
of GCP for data ingestion.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.670
What's unique about
IoT Core is that it's

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:11.460
purpose-built for
bi-directional communication

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.180
and with devices at the edge.

00:26:14.180 --> 00:26:16.680
So just two things-- it does
messaging back and forth

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:21.120
with these devices, and it
provides them a secure identity

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:24.930
that they can use inside the
rest of the GCP ecosystem.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:28.440
Then, the rest of the products
of storage, compute, analytics,

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:31.632
are ones that you might
be familiar with from GCP.

00:26:31.632 --> 00:26:33.090
On the right-hand
side are the ones

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:34.490
that you might be
using for your users.

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:36.480
You might be building
something on App Engine,

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:38.640
or you might, as
we'll hear about,

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:41.070
be building something
using Firebase.

00:26:41.070 --> 00:26:43.800
So to illustrate this
story a little bit better,

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:45.840
I'd like to invite Dallas up.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.840
And Dallas is with
a company called

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:50.790
Owlet, who's gone
through exactly

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:54.065
this kind of consideration
quite recently.

00:26:54.065 --> 00:26:54.940
Thanks a lot, Dallas.

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:03.780
DALLAS HAUETER: All right.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:05.420
Apparently down, not up.

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:08.350
All right, so I am from Owlet.

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:11.310
As you can see here, we're
a health technology company

00:27:11.310 --> 00:27:14.530
founded in 2013.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:18.460
The amazing thing about
Owlet is that we actually

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:23.680
can provide useful information
to parents at the right time.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:26.290
And what we anticipate
being able to do

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:29.650
is to change the way
people or parents

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:34.300
worry about their babies
by being able to tell them

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:36.340
if their baby is OK.

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:41.230
In 2013, our founders
had a problem

00:27:41.230 --> 00:27:42.880
that they wanted
to solve, and that

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.880
was to know if their
babies coming home

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:48.750
from the hospital that
were in the NICU were OK.

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:52.060
And they've been monitored
for months at the NICU,

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:54.340
but when they came home,
they didn't have anything

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:56.140
to tell them if they were OK.

00:27:56.140 --> 00:28:00.430
All of a sudden, they
were on many monitors,

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:04.210
and then overnight, they
didn't have a monitor at all.

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:06.130
And so parents are worried.

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.834
Now, that's one use case.

00:28:07.834 --> 00:28:09.250
And then there's
a use case where,

00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:12.630
everybody knows who has kids,
is my baby still breathing?

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:15.460
And going into the room,
creeping in and kind of seeing

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:18.580
if the baby is breathing and
not getting enough sleep.

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:21.820
And so our product is
able to provide that data

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.130
to customers and
to these parents

00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:26.630
so they get the rest they need.

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:28.480
And it's an amazing
thing that we

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:30.490
can provide for our customers.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:35.350
Right now, last night, there
were over 75,000 babies

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:37.580
being monitored by our monitor.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:40.810
And if you do the math, that's
about 5 billion heartbeats

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:42.850
in a given night
that we are counting,

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:44.670
and we are collecting
all of that data.

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:48.520
We are collecting that data
and putting it into BigQuery

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:54.010
to run analysis on different
types of profiles of babies

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:55.300
that we can find.

00:28:55.300 --> 00:29:02.320
And what we had to do back in
2013 was focus on our product.

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:05.260
We, as a company,
don't want to worry

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:08.200
about the connectivity
engine behind it.

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:11.470
And so we really want
to focus on our product

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:13.984
and what we can provide
to our customers.

00:29:13.984 --> 00:29:16.150
And so I kind of want to
elaborate a little bit more

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:20.020
on how we partition
our problems.

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.510
And before deciding
on a replatforming,

00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:26.050
I'm going to give you a scenario
here with our company history.

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:27.730
Where we first
provided our product,

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:32.300
it was a simple monitor on the
baby's foot to a mobile device.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:33.820
And if anyone knows
hardware, that's

00:29:33.820 --> 00:29:38.957
a Bluetooth module on the
foot to your mobile device.

00:29:38.957 --> 00:29:39.790
Just think about it.

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:41.998
How much range are you going
to get if your signal is

00:29:41.998 --> 00:29:45.400
detuned by a fleshy foot?

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:47.860
Well, we realized--
not as quickly

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:51.460
as we should have-- that we
needed to have a gateway.

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:54.430
And so when we decided that
we needed to have a gateway,

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:57.280
we decided to go with
a technology partner

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:59.950
to help connect our
device to the cloud.

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:03.760
And so with that, we were
able to kind of leave

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:06.040
the connectivity to
our partners and then

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.790
focus on our core product.

00:30:07.790 --> 00:30:14.640
But as we continue
to grow, we really

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:19.054
want to be able to focus on
the next problem we have.

00:30:19.054 --> 00:30:20.220
We want to be able to scale.

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:22.530
We really want a
monitor to go home

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:24.840
with every baby that's going
home from the hospital.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:29.950
And in the US, that's 4
million babies a year.

00:30:29.950 --> 00:30:32.460
And so as we want
to scale, we need

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:38.010
to look at being able to provide
a product that can actually

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:39.810
go to every baby.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:43.380
And the problem
we have right now

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:47.640
is that as we focus
on our product itself,

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:49.740
who's focusing on
the connectivity?

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:54.000
And who's focusing
on that ability

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:57.720
to scale, and to
talk to every parent,

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:00.840
and talk to every monitor?

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:05.820
And so we really want to
now look at our problem

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:09.150
as, how do we solve our
connectivity problem

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:11.385
with our platform?

00:31:11.385 --> 00:31:14.520
Our architecture is
a simple architecture

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.090
that is, I would say, like just
any other connected device,

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:19.830
where you have your
device management

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:22.470
and your authentication,
and you want to pass data

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:25.230
to and from your device.

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:31.860
In our mobile application,
which I'll just kind of show you

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:34.147
here, we actually
provide that data.

00:31:34.147 --> 00:31:35.730
And then from that
mobile application,

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:37.720
you can control the device.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:41.130
And so our goal in
replatforming is

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:47.250
being able to modify our product
on our needs and our schedule.

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:49.200
And so when we look
at our problem,

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:53.730
we say, what isn't
working for us now?

00:31:53.730 --> 00:31:56.100
Instead of trying
to take the whole--

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:58.470
go to the heavy side of
things with your timeline

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:00.180
and try to re-architect
it all at once,

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:02.740
we take one aspect of our
product and move it over.

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:06.120
That makes it better for
us, so that we can actually

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:08.520
meet our mission of
providing information

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.120
to parents at the right time.

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:15.420
And so our first thought there
is, how can we be more agile?

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:18.570
And how can we actually
rely on third parties

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:22.264
as partners in our product, not
just throw it over the fence?

00:32:22.264 --> 00:32:23.430
Here, I need a connectivity!

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:24.060
Throw it back.

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:26.490
And it's just a
generic solution.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:31.515
But how can we utilize third
parties as our partners

00:32:31.515 --> 00:32:33.980
where they support
our mission as well,

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:35.980
and that they can actually
move at our own pace?

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:38.940
And so what we want to do
is take the problem chunk

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:39.670
by chunk.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:45.630
And our first pass was
to take our user database

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:49.800
and bring that under our
control, where we can actually

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:52.110
have better features that
support our customers.

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:57.840
With an out-of-the-box option,
it gets us to market quicker.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:01.350
But it doesn't necessarily
meet the needs of our users

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:02.200
in the long term.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:04.950
And so we take one aspect over.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:06.570
And right now we're
in the process

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:11.130
of migrating our users
to a user database that's

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:12.180
run out of Firebase.

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:15.970
And it was built in Google.

00:33:15.970 --> 00:33:18.750
And so as we take that
on, we can control it,

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.240
and we can modify
it to our needs,

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:26.380
as opposed to relying on a third
party that is out of the box.

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:28.230
And so looking to
the future is being

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:33.450
able to own our own
destiny and develop

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:35.840
a product that meets
our needs, as well

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:37.090
as the needs of our customers.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:39.420
And so by partitioning
it and focusing

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:41.550
on one aspect at
a time, you'll be

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:44.820
able to make
modifications and improve

00:33:44.820 --> 00:33:48.980
your features without causing
angst on your customers.

00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:51.010
Because I can tell you this--

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:56.020
the worst thing about not having
control of your own platform

00:33:56.020 --> 00:34:02.590
is a real scenario with
75,000 moms with babies

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:05.380
who have little sleep,
and all they want to know

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:06.472
is if their baby is OK.

00:34:06.472 --> 00:34:07.930
And instead of
going into the room,

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:11.540
they want to open the app
from the comfort of their bed,

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:14.150
and then they get a message
saying, oh, the device

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:15.650
can't be reached.

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:19.400
Not being able to
control that time

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:22.730
is the worst for
us as a company,

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:24.480
and it's terrible
for our customers.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:28.460
And so we want to be able
to control our platform

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:30.679
and control the
uptime and control

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:34.250
the SLAs and the features that
are there, because what happens

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:37.370
when someone pushes an
update on a generic system

00:34:37.370 --> 00:34:38.730
at 9:00 PM at night?

00:34:38.730 --> 00:34:41.120
And that's when all
of our customers

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:43.969
are trying to access the
app is at 9:00 PM at night.

00:34:43.969 --> 00:34:49.010
And so if we leave that
to somebody else, who

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:52.710
are more of third parties,
it's a detriment to us.

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:57.040
And so we want to be able to
partition that problem as well

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:02.120
and attack it to improve
the customer satisfaction

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:05.000
and improve the
overall information

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:06.717
we can provide to our customers.

00:35:15.896 --> 00:35:17.270
FRASER MACDONALD:
Thanks, Dallas.

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:20.000
And Dallas will be coming back
up in a minute for the Q&amp;A.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:22.160
So questions you've
got about that process,

00:35:22.160 --> 00:35:24.117
he'll be back up here.

00:35:24.117 --> 00:35:25.700
So let's take a look
at one last story

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:29.440
before we have our
panelists come up.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:33.130
And the learning on this
one is follow the ROI.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:36.100
And what we mean by this
is during any conversation

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:38.620
around replatforming,
adding new technology,

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:41.259
moving something to the
cloud, switching providers,

00:35:41.259 --> 00:35:43.300
there's going to be a
point at which you sit down

00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:45.460
and determine, what's it
going to cost us versus

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:46.840
what's it going to gain us?

00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:50.110
And for a lot of IoT products,
the first round of their launch

00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:52.094
is highly experimental.

00:35:52.094 --> 00:35:54.010
I've been surprised at
the amount of customers

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:56.440
that we've talked to who have
been very honest in saying,

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:58.000
the first time we launched
something that was connected,

00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.480
it was just because
our competitor launched

00:35:59.480 --> 00:36:00.874
something that was connected.

00:36:00.874 --> 00:36:02.290
Or, the first time
we launched it,

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:04.232
it was because we thought
it was a cool idea,

00:36:04.232 --> 00:36:06.190
and we wanted to see if
we could figure it out.

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:08.560
But over time, if you get
to the point in maturity

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:10.720
with your product that you're
thinking about a replatforming,

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:13.011
it's because your company
has found some value from it.

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:19.570
Now, this is a statistic that
came out fairly recently, which

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:26.230
is that 1% of gathered data
is properly analyzed and made

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:27.700
use of in IoT devices.

00:36:27.700 --> 00:36:30.089
Which sounds shocking,
but when you consider

00:36:30.089 --> 00:36:31.630
that you're launching
a device that's

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:33.546
connecting to the internet,
your first impulse

00:36:33.546 --> 00:36:35.680
is, let's wire up
absolutely everything

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:37.900
and collect it into a data
lake, and we'll figure out

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:39.092
what to do with it later.

00:36:39.092 --> 00:36:41.050
It becomes a bit clearer
it's not the right way

00:36:41.050 --> 00:36:43.070
to approach the problem.

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:47.110
The next estimate is 75% of
IoT initiatives are failing.

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.690
And that one's even
more surprising.

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:51.970
But they both come
from the same place.

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:53.740
When this is discussing
IoT initiatives,

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:54.970
it's not talking
about products that

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:56.094
are out there in the field.

00:36:56.094 --> 00:36:58.210
It's talking about that
kind of thinking around,

00:36:58.210 --> 00:36:59.860
maybe we should
connect it because!

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:02.290
Or, maybe we should connect
it because our competitor has.

00:37:02.290 --> 00:37:04.081
Or, maybe we should
collect a bunch of data

00:37:04.081 --> 00:37:06.130
and figure out what to
do with it in retrospect.

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:08.255
And they sit in these cycles
where nobody goes back

00:37:08.255 --> 00:37:09.094
for reevaluation.

00:37:09.094 --> 00:37:10.510
And the learning
from this one is,

00:37:10.510 --> 00:37:12.100
if you're at the point where
you're thinking about adding

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:14.620
a new technology, you're
probably also at the point

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:17.736
where you can tell, where's
my business model in here?

00:37:17.736 --> 00:37:20.110
And for a lot of companies,
it comes from different ways.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:21.640
It could be that you've added
a feature that allows you

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:22.810
to sell the product for more.

00:37:22.810 --> 00:37:24.190
It could be that
you've added something

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:26.648
that allows you to differentiate
the product in the market.

00:37:26.648 --> 00:37:28.750
Could be that you've
converted what was previously

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:32.650
a device that people bought
into a service that people had--

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:34.210
recurring basis.

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:37.390
Maybe you figured out how
to make a repair service out

00:37:37.390 --> 00:37:39.804
of whatever your consumer
or commercial product is.

00:37:39.804 --> 00:37:42.220
Or maybe you figured out how
to make a third-party network

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:45.310
of vendors and operators and
repair people and installers

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:47.451
that you can sell
repair data to.

00:37:47.451 --> 00:37:49.450
At the end of the day, a
lot of the IoT products

00:37:49.450 --> 00:37:52.060
that launch and get to
the point of replatforming

00:37:52.060 --> 00:37:53.740
know that in the
back of their heads.

00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:55.156
And so the suggestion,
when you're

00:37:55.156 --> 00:37:58.300
thinking about replatforming,
is follow exactly that.

00:37:58.300 --> 00:37:59.890
Don't go for a wholesale.

00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:02.500
Work on the technology that's
driving where your company's

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:04.610
value is coming from.

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:06.490
So these are some of
the classic use cases

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:09.460
from the ROIs of
a product that you

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:10.810
might want to double down on.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:12.643
You can probably take
a look at your product

00:38:12.643 --> 00:38:15.640
and figure out, which
aspect of this technology

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:18.850
is the one that's really
driving our new feature

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:22.660
or our ability to
deliver a product

00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:25.270
as a service with high
uptime and availability?

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:27.790
Or, is this allowing
us to run our product

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:29.020
at lower cost to ourselves?

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:30.520
So make sure that
your replatforming

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:32.570
is themed around that.

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:36.470
So for a final story, before
we bring up our panelists,

00:38:36.470 --> 00:38:38.080
I want to invite up Paul.

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:41.620
And he's going to
talk to us about a use

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:44.537
case that was exactly like this
with a product called Skykit.

00:38:44.537 --> 00:38:46.445
PAUL LUNDBERG: Thank you.

00:38:46.445 --> 00:38:48.820
Hey, everyone.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:51.720
Paul Lundberg from Agosto.

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:54.510
So we were talking to
a customer one day,

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:55.890
and they basically
started to ask

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:58.600
for what they thought
was an application,

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.720
and what we quickly
viewed as a product.

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:04.500
And so we went out to try to
help them find the product,

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:07.470
because it didn't make sense
to build it for one customer.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:10.239
And we found that what
they were asking for just

00:39:10.239 --> 00:39:11.530
wasn't out there in the market.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:13.890
And so we couldn't
help ourselves

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:17.280
but to start to investigate
this and to go down

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:19.740
the path of starting
to build out what

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:21.030
we thought could be a product.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:24.330
So it's a digital
signage solution

00:39:24.330 --> 00:39:29.760
that is built entirely on
Google and uses Chrome OS

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.420
as the endpoint media player.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.660
So there's a lot
of people out there

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:38.580
that love Chrome OS for
its security and lower cost

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:40.650
and manageability.

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:42.680
And so we started to
go down that path.

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:44.430
We had a lot of
experience with Chrome OS.

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:47.520
We had a lot of experience
developing on Google's cloud.

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:50.960
And there were only
600 other companies

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:52.680
with digital signage products.

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:53.690
So we thought, why not?

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:56.400
We'll jump in and give it a go.

00:39:56.400 --> 00:40:00.810
So based on Chrome
OS, we started

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:02.440
with a microservices
architecture--

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:04.500
I'll go into a little
more detail on that--

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:06.810
entirely built on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:08.820
And I'll get into
this a little more.

00:40:08.820 --> 00:40:13.350
Some of the learnings that
we really kind of already

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:16.480
knew from doing product
development for other companies

00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:20.730
is that building a product,
it's continual redevelopment.

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:24.060
I mean, the technology changes.

00:40:24.060 --> 00:40:25.350
The requirements change.

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:29.250
The scaling of the
product changes.

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:32.940
And so you're continually
just looking at, how do we--

00:40:32.940 --> 00:40:35.490
and as everyone has talked about
so far that's been up here,

00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:39.000
how do we incrementally
move this product forward?

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:42.450
And you know, we've got
the plane in the air,

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:44.820
and we have to do this live.

00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:48.300
So let me just show
you roughly what

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:50.620
our very high-level
architecture looks like.

00:40:50.620 --> 00:40:56.160
So in the big blue box here, we
have a few of the microservices

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:57.480
that we built out initially.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:41:01.620
One was responsible
for managing content.

00:41:01.620 --> 00:41:05.550
Customers create
content, format content,

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:08.670
and they want to schedule
that to play on players,

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:11.970
put it in playlists, set
all kinds of rules and tags

00:41:11.970 --> 00:41:14.050
about where that's
going to play.

00:41:14.050 --> 00:41:17.340
And then Skykit Admin Service
is another microservice

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:20.730
that is all about abstracting
the device management

00:41:20.730 --> 00:41:23.760
and make it really
straightforward for customers

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:27.030
to manage their
fleet of devices.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:30.030
Behind the scenes, we're
interfacing with Chrome device

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:34.650
management, interfacing
with a logging service.

00:41:34.650 --> 00:41:38.670
And largely, this
architecture is

00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:40.380
very much like a
mobile architecture,

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:43.650
in that we couldn't always count
on our devices being online.

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:45.270
So we had to think about that.

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:49.770
In retail, or many of the
places where these devices go,

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:52.730
connectivity is an
issue in terms of Wi-Fi.

00:41:52.730 --> 00:41:53.410
It's going down.

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:54.630
It's coming back.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:57.510
We need to be able to
always play the content.

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:00.870
When we have the opportunity,
pull the content down.

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:03.270
Be ready to play whatever
updated content there

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:04.610
is that's available.

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:08.370
So we had a microservices
architecture,

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:09.640
devices on the edge.

00:42:09.640 --> 00:42:11.250
We were doing push
notifications using

00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:15.600
Google Cloud Messaging, very
much a mobile architecture

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:16.360
platform.

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:22.560
And so then, at that time,
when we built this product,

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:25.050
Google's cloud platform
was very different

00:42:25.050 --> 00:42:26.890
in terms of the
services available.

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:30.840
So as an example, we didn't
have Pub/Sub available to us.

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:36.480
And IoT Core was not available
yet to handle MQTT messaging.

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:39.540
So when those started
to come around,

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:42.300
and as we were building our
own experience around IoT

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:45.000
and other projects, we
started to really think

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:48.600
about this product as
being really an IoT

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:51.630
product in that we've got
a lot of devices out there

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:53.410
that we're trying to manage.

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:54.960
And so we started
thinking about,

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:58.530
how do we start to move
this architecture into more

00:42:58.530 --> 00:43:00.570
of an IoT pattern?

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:02.760
And how do we
leverage the services

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:05.590
that are available
on Google's cloud?

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:08.560
So this isn't a picture of
the entire architecture.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:14.950
This is really drilling down
into more of a view of IoT.

00:43:14.950 --> 00:43:16.420
So we have our
Skykit player, which

00:43:16.420 --> 00:43:21.070
is a media player, HDMI
cable driving a panel.

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:22.810
A big change is
that everything we

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:27.460
were doing up until the
new architecture was HTTP.

00:43:27.460 --> 00:43:33.850
And MQTT is a lower-level
protocol, very good for IoT,

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:35.620
in that we can
maintain a connection

00:43:35.620 --> 00:43:40.660
as long as we have it and have
traffic flowing both ways.

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:43.810
It's a much lighter protocol
in terms of the overhead.

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:50.230
And it also gives us, with
IoT Core, a much better model

00:43:50.230 --> 00:43:53.050
for security, in
terms of us securing

00:43:53.050 --> 00:43:56.110
each individual device,
and really turning

00:43:56.110 --> 00:43:58.510
some of that responsibility
over to Google,

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:03.670
in terms of their underlying
IoT Core offering.

00:44:03.670 --> 00:44:07.750
So our general philosophy
in developing products is--

00:44:07.750 --> 00:44:11.620
use what's available as core
components on Google's cloud

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:13.840
and build what you have to.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.940
And when Google comes
out with a service,

00:44:15.940 --> 00:44:17.530
where they're taking
responsibility

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:20.200
for the scaling and
all the complexities,

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:27.070
then move to that, and don't
support that code anymore

00:44:27.070 --> 00:44:28.690
that you developed
in a custom way.

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:32.830
So what's really important
with the new architecture

00:44:32.830 --> 00:44:35.470
is that now that our product's
becoming more complex,

00:44:35.470 --> 00:44:40.030
and we're looking
at adding to it.

00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:42.280
Instead of just a
couple microservices

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:45.942
that are communicating
between one another,

00:44:45.942 --> 00:44:47.650
we have more microservices
that are going

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:49.000
to be coming into the picture.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:54.310
And what's really great
about IoT Core and Pub/Sub

00:44:54.310 --> 00:44:57.820
working in conjunction
is that as messages

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:00.490
come into the system
through IoT Core

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:03.310
and get put onto
Pub/Sub queues, we

00:45:03.310 --> 00:45:07.820
can have multiple
subscribers to that data.

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:11.290
So now, if, for
example, we have,

00:45:11.290 --> 00:45:15.160
let's say, player state, what's
going on with the player,

00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:19.900
coming in through Cloud Pub/Sub,
both the Skykit admin, which

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:23.920
is responsible for devices,
and the content manager,

00:45:23.920 --> 00:45:26.590
where we want to show
in the UI to a user what

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:29.140
the state of a device is, we
can just have both of them

00:45:29.140 --> 00:45:30.190
subscribe and show that.

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:32.715
We don't have to have it
come in through Skykit admin

00:45:32.715 --> 00:45:34.090
and then make sure
we communicate

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:36.520
that to content manager.

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:38.780
So it's a much more
scalable solution.

00:45:38.780 --> 00:45:40.960
It also gives us
the opportunity to--

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:44.590
as an example, let's take data
and just move it into BigQuery.

00:45:44.590 --> 00:45:48.340
Let's have multiple
subscribers, where we can just

00:45:48.340 --> 00:45:49.990
have another
subscriber to that data

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:53.920
and start to look
and think about how

00:45:53.920 --> 00:45:56.810
we can use that stream of
data in a different way.

00:45:56.810 --> 00:46:00.400
So our learnings
out of all of this

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:06.370
were just continual
redevelopment--

00:46:06.370 --> 00:46:08.920
have that kind of mindset.

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.400
We're also trying to position
our product so that we

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:13.540
have a lot of flexibility.

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:15.550
So where's the ROI?

00:46:15.550 --> 00:46:17.140
We don't know exactly.

00:46:17.140 --> 00:46:19.600
But we know that we want to
be as flexible as possible.

00:46:19.600 --> 00:46:23.240
So as an example, we had
a customer come to us--

00:46:23.240 --> 00:46:25.320
in fact, now our
largest customer--

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:29.950
that said, well, we really
like your Skykit product.

00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:31.150
It's built on Chrome.

00:46:31.150 --> 00:46:35.890
Problem is, we've got 10,000
devices deployed today that are

00:46:35.890 --> 00:46:38.210
running on an Android device.

00:46:38.210 --> 00:46:42.610
So what we did then was to
rebuild just that player

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:43.111
component.

00:46:43.111 --> 00:46:45.110
If you remember the bigger
architecture picture,

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:46.490
the player is just
one component.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:49.750
We were able to
redevelop that in Android

00:46:49.750 --> 00:46:53.260
and connect it and
get that working,

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:55.180
so that we could win that deal.

00:46:55.180 --> 00:46:57.610
So we don't know exactly
what the future holds.

00:46:57.610 --> 00:47:00.460
But we know that if we
can architect our system

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:04.840
to be as flexible as possible,
that another opportunity might

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:08.200
be a panel provider that
says we have a system on chip

00:47:08.200 --> 00:47:11.317
in our panel, and if you could
develop it in a certain way,

00:47:11.317 --> 00:47:13.150
then we could run your
product, and we could

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:14.824
help you sell your product.

00:47:14.824 --> 00:47:16.990
When we have a big deal,
we could bring that to you.

00:47:16.990 --> 00:47:23.140
So we know that by being
as flexible as possible

00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:25.510
and having an
architecture that supports

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:30.250
us moving quickly and supporting
as many different possibilities

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:34.440
as we can, that we're
likely to have success.

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:39.133
So Fraser, I think you're
going to open up the panel.

00:47:39.133 --> 00:47:42.080
FRASER MACDONALD: Sounds great.

00:47:42.080 --> 00:47:43.277
OK.

00:47:43.277 --> 00:47:44.860
I think we'll keep
using this one too.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:45.760
Thanks a lot, Paul.

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:48.389
So I'm going to invite a
couple panelists up now.

00:47:48.389 --> 00:47:49.930
One is Dallas, who
you've heard from.

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:51.952
And Paul, representing Skykit.

00:47:51.952 --> 00:47:53.410
And as well, we've
got the pleasure

00:47:53.410 --> 00:47:57.110
of welcoming Verne up from ADO.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:47:59.770
So these are three
people who have

00:47:59.770 --> 00:48:01.660
gone through
replatformings themselves,

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:04.850
and their companies have taken
a look at the ups and downs

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:05.860
and walked through it.

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:07.900
And we'll get a chance to ask
them some practical questions

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:08.696
from it.

00:48:08.696 --> 00:48:11.004
I'll hand some mics to you guys.

00:48:15.350 --> 00:48:18.719
So I'll kick us off with a
question for each of you.

00:48:18.719 --> 00:48:21.260
And Verne, if you want to take
a moment to introduce yourself

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.480
as we go along as well.

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:25.100
The first question
that I'm wondering

00:48:25.100 --> 00:48:26.810
is, what was that moment like?

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:30.620
Because a lot of replatforming
products start with the worry

00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:32.745
that you're wasting
your time, or you're

00:48:32.745 --> 00:48:34.370
spending it inefficiently,
or something

00:48:34.370 --> 00:48:35.949
could go wrong along the way.

00:48:35.949 --> 00:48:38.240
What was the moment that
really tipped the scales, that

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:40.580
told you, no, this
investment is worth

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:42.110
what we're putting into it?

00:48:42.110 --> 00:48:43.520
VERNE LIZANO POVEDANO: OK.

00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:46.040
Good afternoon.

00:48:46.040 --> 00:48:50.210
Well, we started with this
project about 12 years ago.

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.630
We decided to create a
kind of open platform

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:57.140
that can be used for the
telematics of the group.

00:48:57.140 --> 00:49:05.900
So we started installing all
of this in about 4,000 buses.

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:08.100
Over the years, we have
been working with this.

00:49:08.100 --> 00:49:13.180
But at some point, we were not
able to construct more things

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:14.090
over this platform.

00:49:14.090 --> 00:49:20.390
So it became something
that was a black box,

00:49:20.390 --> 00:49:24.380
where we cannot
do something new.

00:49:24.380 --> 00:49:27.560
When you came with
us, we discovered

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:31.580
that we had an opportunity
for changing things.

00:49:31.580 --> 00:49:35.720
And the first step
that we did is

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:41.180
to try to create a small
bullet tracer, as you said.

00:49:41.180 --> 00:49:44.960
And with this bullet
tracer, we identified

00:49:44.960 --> 00:49:48.680
that there was no
risk, no real risk,

00:49:48.680 --> 00:49:56.330
in following this methodology
and in creating an IoT

00:49:56.330 --> 00:50:03.540
platform, an open platform,
in the existing hardware.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:06.410
So right now we feel
pretty confident that it

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:09.080
is the time to scale this.

00:50:09.080 --> 00:50:11.300
And we hope that in
the following months

00:50:11.300 --> 00:50:14.420
we're going to be
able to set up all

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:17.450
of this platform
in some more buses.

00:50:17.450 --> 00:50:19.250
So we are ready for that.

00:50:19.250 --> 00:50:21.470
We feel more confident.

00:50:21.470 --> 00:50:23.100
I'm still working
for the company.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:27.320
So I'm not getting fired yet.

00:50:27.320 --> 00:50:29.510
And I'm pretty
sure it's not going

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:34.010
to happen because we feel
we are in very good hands.

00:50:34.010 --> 00:50:40.520
And the methodology is
one that is telling you,

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:43.630
you can go to the next step
and to the other steps.

00:50:43.630 --> 00:50:46.880
So we feel very
confident on that, yes.

00:50:52.902 --> 00:50:55.110
FRASER MACDONALD: It's the
same just for each of you.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:57.210
What was that moment
like that tipped

00:50:57.210 --> 00:51:01.010
the scale for a replatforming?

00:51:01.010 --> 00:51:04.310
DALLAS HAUETER: I think it's
been an evolution over the five

00:51:04.310 --> 00:51:06.350
years that Owlet
has been around.

00:51:06.350 --> 00:51:10.040
As I said when I spoke
earlier, that as we focused

00:51:10.040 --> 00:51:14.000
on the beginning, on
launching our pulse oximetry,

00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:16.970
and that data that we're trying
to provide to our customers,

00:51:16.970 --> 00:51:20.330
once we kind of got our
feet underneath us on that,

00:51:20.330 --> 00:51:24.140
we then started seeing
where else we could improve.

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:28.610
And that's the next
part of our product

00:51:28.610 --> 00:51:32.120
is really how the data
gets to the customer.

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:34.110
We can provide the data now.

00:51:34.110 --> 00:51:36.260
We have accurate data.

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:41.510
And so once we were
confident that that is there,

00:51:41.510 --> 00:51:44.420
obviously we'll improve
that even of itself.

00:51:44.420 --> 00:51:45.920
But we started seeing--

00:51:45.920 --> 00:51:48.810
we call it the forest--

00:51:48.810 --> 00:51:50.750
we start chopping
down the big trees.

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:53.180
And then you start seeing
what else is growing up.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:56.420
And the biggest tree
next was our platform.

00:51:56.420 --> 00:51:59.180
And so that's what
we're doing is

00:51:59.180 --> 00:52:02.910
we're taking one
tree down at a time.

00:52:02.910 --> 00:52:06.350
PAUL LUNDBERG: And I'd answer
that by saying that initially,

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:08.330
when we're building
the product, we're not

00:52:08.330 --> 00:52:10.340
thinking about scale as much.

00:52:10.340 --> 00:52:13.430
We just want to see it work for
a customer, two customers, 10

00:52:13.430 --> 00:52:14.910
customers.

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:17.570
And so we're focused a
lot on the application

00:52:17.570 --> 00:52:18.770
and is it functioning.

00:52:18.770 --> 00:52:21.710
And is it doing what
the customer's wanting

00:52:21.710 --> 00:52:22.820
and expecting it to do?

00:52:22.820 --> 00:52:24.350
Does it have the right features?

00:52:24.350 --> 00:52:25.940
Can we compete?

00:52:25.940 --> 00:52:28.340
And then the
tipping point for us

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:33.017
is really when your primary
worry is about scale.

00:52:33.017 --> 00:52:34.100
So the product is working.

00:52:34.100 --> 00:52:35.730
Customers are
reasonably satisfied.

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:38.630
There's an endless road
map of features to build.

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:41.840
But the thing that could really
get us is if we can't scale,

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:46.400
and if we can't move quickly
to address concerns as we

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:49.200
see them, in terms of scaling.

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:53.370
And with this type of solution,
it's about device management.

00:52:53.370 --> 00:52:57.020
It's about having the
data to answer questions.

00:52:57.020 --> 00:53:02.390
It's about having good data
about device state and things

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:03.800
like that.

00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:06.660
So when we really started to
think about re-architecting,

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:10.640
it was really around,
the product works.

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:11.750
It looks like it's a go.

00:53:11.750 --> 00:53:13.210
But how are we going to scale?

00:53:15.375 --> 00:53:17.500
FRASER MACDONALD: So I'll
take a second, if there's

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:19.720
a question from the audience,
before another question

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:21.470
for our panelists.

00:53:21.470 --> 00:53:25.880
There's a microphone
just up here for anyone.

00:53:25.880 --> 00:53:29.830
So I'll ask you a
question, Verne, about ADO.

00:53:29.830 --> 00:53:33.460
You had a decision to
migrate your back office

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:36.010
for your operations
to Google Cloud.

00:53:36.010 --> 00:53:38.500
And what do you
think was the biggest

00:53:38.500 --> 00:53:41.510
impact of moving your back
office from where it was before

00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:43.447
into a cloud-based operation?

00:53:43.447 --> 00:53:44.530
VERNE LIZANO POVEDANO: OK.

00:53:44.530 --> 00:53:50.320
The first one was about cost,
because our platform is based--

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:54.190
typically the standard platform
based on local computers,

00:53:54.190 --> 00:53:55.930
local servers.

00:53:55.930 --> 00:53:58.900
And this is very,
very expensive.

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:03.550
Any time you require some more
processing computing power

00:54:03.550 --> 00:54:08.860
or some data storage, you need
to buy some more computers.

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:12.160
This is not anymore the
case with the cloud.

00:54:12.160 --> 00:54:18.370
So in terms of cost, it
has a huge, huge impact.

00:54:18.370 --> 00:54:20.380
The other one is about ubiquity.

00:54:20.380 --> 00:54:23.560
We have operations in different
cities around the world.

00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:27.700
And the thing here is that
it is absolutely wonderful

00:54:27.700 --> 00:54:30.970
not having to go to
Spain or other countries

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:33.220
and installing infrastructure.

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:35.670
Since everything is
already in the cloud,

00:54:35.670 --> 00:54:38.650
you're able to go anywhere.

00:54:38.650 --> 00:54:41.860
And the third thing
is about connectivity.

00:54:41.860 --> 00:54:44.980
Most of our customers
are not only the ones

00:54:44.980 --> 00:54:49.960
who are traveling with us in
the buses but also the cities.

00:54:49.960 --> 00:54:52.540
And the cities today, we
all know that what they want

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:54.470
is open data.

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:59.230
So the connectivity provided
by the cloud is amazing.

00:54:59.230 --> 00:55:01.960
And we don't have to do
anything by ourselves.

00:55:01.960 --> 00:55:03.730
We don't need to
develop new technology.

00:55:03.730 --> 00:55:05.620
We don't need to
develop anything

00:55:05.620 --> 00:55:10.360
for providing this open
data to the cities.

00:55:10.360 --> 00:55:12.570
FRASER MACDONALD: Thanks.

00:55:12.570 --> 00:55:14.910
So for you, Dallas,
on a specific point,

00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:17.700
you're in the middle
of migrating your user

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:18.990
applications.

00:55:18.990 --> 00:55:20.580
And you mentioned
that you've never

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:23.130
really seen a
difficult-to-please group

00:55:23.130 --> 00:55:26.370
of users until you've seen
tired, frustrated parents who

00:55:26.370 --> 00:55:28.860
just want to make
sure their baby is OK.

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:30.780
So this is a really
strategic, but it's also

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:32.300
a really sensitive
project for you.

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:33.450
So as you were
going into it, what

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:35.116
were some of the
considerations that you

00:55:35.116 --> 00:55:38.199
had about migrating
user applications

00:55:38.199 --> 00:55:39.990
and mobile applications
on something that's

00:55:39.990 --> 00:55:42.760
already out there in the field?

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:45.400
DALLAS HAUETER: So that's
a very good question, when

00:55:45.400 --> 00:55:48.460
it comes to, how do you
handle stressful parents?

00:55:48.460 --> 00:55:50.680
And so I think
what we're doing is

00:55:50.680 --> 00:55:54.690
we're trying to release it at
the same time as new features.

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:56.390
And so at this time
we launch this,

00:55:56.390 --> 00:55:58.780
we're actually launching
a brand-new product.

00:55:58.780 --> 00:56:02.140
And we're also
going into the UK,

00:56:02.140 --> 00:56:03.850
coming at the end of the year.

00:56:03.850 --> 00:56:08.350
And with that move, we're
going to kind of marry that

00:56:08.350 --> 00:56:11.500
with the improvements for GDPR.

00:56:11.500 --> 00:56:14.170
And so if we were
just to say, hey,

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:17.300
because it's a pain right
now with our platform,

00:56:17.300 --> 00:56:18.580
let's move over.

00:56:18.580 --> 00:56:21.550
It's kind of hard to sell
the reason why you have

00:56:21.550 --> 00:56:23.560
to set up your device again.

00:56:23.560 --> 00:56:25.900
Essentially, this
replatforming, unfortunately,

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:30.100
causes the old system
to not work, and then

00:56:30.100 --> 00:56:32.280
the new system to take over.

00:56:32.280 --> 00:56:36.160
So we don't want to upset
our parents, our customers,

00:56:36.160 --> 00:56:37.720
too much.

00:56:37.720 --> 00:56:39.950
But we're also not
going to force them.

00:56:39.950 --> 00:56:42.070
So on this move,
this replatforming,

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:44.390
it's a slow migration.

00:56:44.390 --> 00:56:46.780
We will actually
have our product

00:56:46.780 --> 00:56:50.350
in the current app still
available for six months.

00:56:50.350 --> 00:56:53.540
And then we'll push our
customers towards it.

00:56:53.540 --> 00:56:57.370
So they choose when they
do the replatforming,

00:56:57.370 --> 00:57:01.360
so that it's not
a critical time.

00:57:01.360 --> 00:57:03.580
Sometimes parents
aren't using the app

00:57:03.580 --> 00:57:06.640
until they get a notification
that something's wrong.

00:57:06.640 --> 00:57:09.730
And so it would be
the worst experience

00:57:09.730 --> 00:57:11.821
to get a notification
on your device,

00:57:11.821 --> 00:57:14.320
and then go to your app, and
it's like, oh, it doesn't work.

00:57:14.320 --> 00:57:14.819
Sorry.

00:57:14.819 --> 00:57:16.390
You needed to
download the new one.

00:57:16.390 --> 00:57:18.500
So we don't want that to happen.

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:20.960
And so it's two-sided.

00:57:20.960 --> 00:57:24.280
We marry it with new features,
new products, hey, new things!

00:57:24.280 --> 00:57:27.370
Here, now let's replatform.

00:57:27.370 --> 00:57:33.320
And then also don't push
them, and do a slow rollout.

00:57:33.320 --> 00:57:34.770
FRASER MACDONALD: Great.

00:57:34.770 --> 00:57:35.270
Thanks.

00:57:35.270 --> 00:57:38.430
And Paul, a question for
you on customers as well.

00:57:38.430 --> 00:57:41.030
You talked a bit about how the
technology changes you made

00:57:41.030 --> 00:57:42.530
allowed you to roll
out in new sites

00:57:42.530 --> 00:57:44.300
and with new pieces of hardware.

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:46.610
What about on the
customer side of things?

00:57:46.610 --> 00:57:48.560
How do you sort of
draw the dotted line

00:57:48.560 --> 00:57:52.100
between technical improvements
and customer happiness

00:57:52.100 --> 00:57:54.250
for Skykit?

00:57:54.250 --> 00:57:56.550
PAUL LUNDBERG: So Skykit's
a software as a service,

00:57:56.550 --> 00:58:00.350
so our customers really don't
know or necessarily understand

00:58:00.350 --> 00:58:03.830
the details about how
we deliver our product.

00:58:03.830 --> 00:58:05.810
So for us, it's just
about dependability.

00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:10.670
So early on, we had some issues
with players being offline,

00:58:10.670 --> 00:58:13.100
for example, with no
connectivity, where

00:58:13.100 --> 00:58:15.160
in our application,
it would say, well,

00:58:15.160 --> 00:58:18.460
our last known state
is that it was up.

00:58:18.460 --> 00:58:22.490
But there were delays of
where state would be correct.

00:58:22.490 --> 00:58:26.330
So it's really about, for
making an architecture that's

00:58:26.330 --> 00:58:28.310
going to make the
product better,

00:58:28.310 --> 00:58:31.820
is one that is going
to provide accurate

00:58:31.820 --> 00:58:33.710
information about
exactly what's going

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:38.172
on with the devices in a
fairly complex architecture.

00:58:38.172 --> 00:58:39.130
FRASER MACDONALD: Sure.

00:58:39.130 --> 00:58:40.250
Thanks.

00:58:40.250 --> 00:58:42.850
So we have time for a
question from the audience,

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:43.790
if we have one.

00:58:43.790 --> 00:58:45.950
And if not, I'll wrap
up with a question

00:58:45.950 --> 00:58:47.290
for each of our panelists here.

00:58:47.290 --> 00:58:48.706
And thank you again
for your time.

00:58:51.380 --> 00:58:52.080
OK.

00:58:52.080 --> 00:58:54.767
If everyone's set
here, so a finishing

00:58:54.767 --> 00:58:56.100
question for our panelists here.

00:58:56.100 --> 00:58:57.974
You've all gone through,
or are in the middle

00:58:57.974 --> 00:58:59.569
of a replatforming
process yourself.

00:58:59.569 --> 00:59:01.110
And some of the
folks in the audience

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:02.651
are curious about
exactly that, which

00:59:02.651 --> 00:59:04.000
is why they're at this talk.

00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:07.140
If you had to offer a tip
up to another company that

00:59:07.140 --> 00:59:09.210
is thinking about the
process right now,

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:12.540
or knows it might come down the
road, what would be something

00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:13.041
you'd say?

00:59:13.041 --> 00:59:15.206
Now, that I'm on the other
side, I wish I knew that.

00:59:15.206 --> 00:59:17.250
What would be your tip
for a company thinking

00:59:17.250 --> 00:59:19.440
about a replatform?

00:59:19.440 --> 00:59:21.120
Paul, I'll hand
off to you first.

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:22.860
PAUL LUNDBERG: OK, sure.

00:59:22.860 --> 00:59:26.640
Well, I would say
that a theme I think

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:29.190
you've heard with all these
examples, and all our stories,

00:59:29.190 --> 00:59:30.690
are that you want
to figure out how

00:59:30.690 --> 00:59:34.530
to incrementally go after this.

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:37.560
I think it's important
to just identify,

00:59:37.560 --> 00:59:40.470
what do we go after first?

00:59:40.470 --> 00:59:44.760
And there can be a lot of
different ways to look at that.

00:59:44.760 --> 00:59:47.610
One, I think one of
the examples was, well,

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:51.990
let's look for where we
have a big problem, where

00:59:51.990 --> 00:59:55.320
replacing that part
of the application

00:59:55.320 --> 00:59:59.130
could really help us.

00:59:59.130 --> 01:00:01.680
Another might be to just pick
something straightforward,

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:04.170
to get things underway.

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:10.290
A lot of times, you need to
set this up and convince people

01:00:10.290 --> 01:00:13.110
other than the technical team
that there's an investment

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:15.345
to be made, and you want
to show some success.

01:00:18.370 --> 01:00:20.760
So there's lots of
different reasons.

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:23.780
But those are a few I'd offer.

01:00:23.780 --> 01:00:27.990
DALLAS HAUETER: What I would
offer is, in any company,

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:29.560
you're trying to
grow your business.

01:00:29.560 --> 01:00:34.290
So if you know it's coming,
it's inevitable, don't wait.

01:00:34.290 --> 01:00:35.220
Start now.

01:00:35.220 --> 01:00:38.130
And that's, granted, take
it by piece by piece.

01:00:38.130 --> 01:00:42.840
But start now, because
the more and more angry

01:00:42.840 --> 01:00:45.370
parents that you have with
the current situation,

01:00:45.370 --> 01:00:47.040
it just gets worse and worse.

01:00:47.040 --> 01:00:48.810
And so you want to start now.

01:00:48.810 --> 01:00:50.100
Please your customers.

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:51.780
And do the best that you can.

01:00:51.780 --> 01:00:53.310
We're going to do
that with the UK.

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:57.330
Instead of going and launching
our old system on it,

01:00:57.330 --> 01:00:59.550
we're going to launch
the new system.

01:00:59.550 --> 01:01:02.430
Like, just restaging
it out, because we

01:01:02.430 --> 01:01:04.260
would get into the same boat.

01:01:04.260 --> 01:01:05.947
If we launch UK
with our old system,

01:01:05.947 --> 01:01:07.530
we're going to have
the same problems.

01:01:07.530 --> 01:01:09.240
We're going to
have to replatform.

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:11.310
So launch now.

01:01:11.310 --> 01:01:12.210
So start now.

01:01:14.544 --> 01:01:16.710
VERNE LIZANO POVEDANO: Well,
I guess the first thing

01:01:16.710 --> 01:01:20.190
is to ask the right question.

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:23.130
What is the reason
for replatforming?

01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:28.611
Is this because you have
technology that is becoming

01:01:28.611 --> 01:01:29.110
older?

01:01:29.110 --> 01:01:32.130
Technology is going to
become older any time.

01:01:32.130 --> 01:01:35.150
Since the first deployment
you have, after this,

01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:38.010
it's going to be
older and older.

01:01:38.010 --> 01:01:39.990
So I guess the
right question here

01:01:39.990 --> 01:01:44.700
is, what is the service,
what is the kind of service

01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:47.940
that I want to propose to
my customers in the future,

01:01:47.940 --> 01:01:50.730
knowing that the world
is changing very fast?

01:01:50.730 --> 01:01:54.390
In our case, the
question was, what

01:01:54.390 --> 01:01:58.550
is the competitiveness that I'm
going to have with my customer

01:01:58.550 --> 01:02:01.620
cities in the future
if I don't have an IoT

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:06.100
platform for offering
them new possibilities?

01:02:06.100 --> 01:02:10.740
So in our case, it's not
because of technology.

01:02:10.740 --> 01:02:12.870
It's because we are
not going to have

01:02:12.870 --> 01:02:15.720
something valuable
to propose them

01:02:15.720 --> 01:02:17.890
in the very, very near future.

01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:19.760
So that's why we are
starting right now.

01:02:19.760 --> 01:02:24.127
It's a question of
survival of the business.

01:02:24.127 --> 01:02:25.960
FRASER MACDONALD: Well,
Paul, Dallas, Verne,

01:02:25.960 --> 01:02:27.520
thank you again for joining us.

01:02:27.520 --> 01:02:29.770
And to everyone in the
audience, thank you.

